    "['peer-as [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.2.1.1 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nneighbor 10.2.1.1 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper command `set protocols bgp group external-peers peer-as 100` sets the remote AS number for all neighbors in the \"external-peers\" group to 100.\\n- In Cisco, we specify the AS number per neighbor using the `neighbor [ip] remote-as [as-number]` command.\\n- Since the neighbor IP (10.2.1.1) was defined in a separate Juniper command, we include it in the Cisco translation.\\n- The command establishes that the BGP neighbor at 10.2.1.1 belongs to AS 100.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.2.1.1 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP neighbor configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.1 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, we first enter BGP configuration mode with the local AS number (100 in this case, taken from the Juniper's `peer-as` setting)\\n2. Then we configure the neighbor with their IP address and remote AS number\\n3. The AS numbers are the same (100) because this appears to be an eBGP peer relationship where the Juniper router (AS 100) is peering with another router in the same AS\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.2.1.1 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP neighbor configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.1 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, we first enter BGP configuration mode with the local AS number (100 in this case, taken from the Juniper's `peer-as` setting)\\n2. Then we configure the neighbor with their IP address and remote AS number\\n3. The AS numbers are the same (100) because this appears to be an eBGP peer relationship where the Juniper router (AS 100) is peering with another router in the same AS\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.2.1.1 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
